##
    A set changed while a 'for each' was walking it, reached under a second name.

    This program compiles and then fails. Changing the sequence a 'for each' names is refused
    while compiling — see samples/negatives/compile/looping.pc — but that refusal matches the
    name the loop wrote, and here the set is reached through a parameter instead.

    No local rule can see that: what Drain does with what it is handed is Drain's business, and
    the set could equally be stored in a field or handed on again. So the set itself knows a
    walk is running, and refuses whatever path the change arrives by.

    The same division as dividing by zero, which is a compile error where the zero is visible
    and an exception where it is not.
##

model Basket

    public integer[] contents;

    public function Basket()
        this.contents = {1, 2, 3};
    end function

    # Innocent enough on its own. What makes it a mistake is where it is called from.
    public function Drain(integer[] items)
        items.Clear();
    end function
end model

global model Program
    function Main()
        Basket basket = new Basket();

        for each item in basket.contents
            Console.WriteLine(item);
            basket.Drain(basket.contents);
        end for

        Console.WriteLine("never reached");
    end function
end model
